        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best walking pad for tall people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most tall walkers, a pad with at least a 47-inch belt, such as the WalkingPad Z1R, which folds and fits under a desk. On a budget, the WalkingPad Z1 has a 47.6-inch walking area. For long workdays or heavier users, the LifeSpan TR5000Pro-GlowUp and TX6-GlowUp have 20 by 48-inch belts.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a walking pad belt be for a tall person?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As a rule of thumb, look for 47 inches or more if you are about 6 feet or taller. The belt needs roughly one step length plus one shoe length plus a few inches of margin. For a 6-foot-2 walker that is around 45 to 50 inches depending on pace. Measure your own step length to check.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 40-inch walking pad long enough at 6 feet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At a slow amble it can be, but many people around 6 feet will find it cramped at a brisk pace. By the rule of thumb on this page, a 6-foot walker's step plus a shoe is already in the low to mid 40s of inches, so a 40-inch belt pushes you to shorten your stride. A 47-inch belt is the safer choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a tall person use a walking pad under a standing desk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, but check the desk first. A walking pad raises you by 4 to 7 inches, and many standing desks stop around 47 or 48 inches. A 6-foot-4 walker on a 5-inch pad needs a desk surface around 54 inches, by our desk-height formula. Low-deck pads such as the LifeSpan models at about 4.6 inches help.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should a tall person buy a walking pad or a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If you only walk at desk speeds and are under about 6 foot 4, a long-belt walking pad is usually enough. If you are taller and walk briskly, want to jog or run, or want steep incline, a treadmill with a 55 to 60-inch deck is the better machine. A folding treadmill is often the compromise when space is tight.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are walking pad belt lengths accurate?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, but read them carefully. Brands quote belt or walking area, while some marketplace listings quote the whole machine, which is about 56 to 58 inches on these pads. Metric conversions cause small differences, and different versions under similar names cause large ones. Check the model number and the brand's own page.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

